lisa-design:
- Option 2 of the spec (generated except-paths), not option 1: Regal 0.43.0 has no way to read data files. docs/rules/imports/unresolved-reference.md says "Regal does not scan any data files"; lint input is filtered to .rego/.rq (internal/io/files/filter/filter.go NotRego, used by pkg/config/filter.go); `regal lint` has no data flag; project.roots entries only carry path + rego-version (pkg/config/config.go Root).
- Patterns are `data.<prefix>.<key>` plus `data.<prefix>.<key>.**` per top-level key of each data.json/data.yaml/data.yml under the bundle root, where <prefix> is the file's directory relative to the bundle root (OPA bundle semantics). Typos in data keys and refs to missing packages still error; refs below a data key are not checked (inherent: Regal has no data).
- Keys/dir segments that aren't identifiers are skipped: Regal's ref_to_string renders them as ["..."] and the rule cuts the ref at the first "[", so they never reach except-paths matching; skipping also avoids glob metacharacters in patterns.
- The generated config is only built for the shared config (SHARED=true); repos with their own .regal config keep it untouched (spec rule 3). except-paths is appended (+=) so a future shared except-paths list isn't overwritten; level is not set so the provided default (error) stays.
- Logic stays in the regal lint step (yq/jq, both preinstalled on ubuntu-latest) rather than a Go cmd: the job runs in the caller's checkout with no Go toolchain or workflows checkout beyond the sparse config; covered by internal/ciworkflows/regal_test.go step tests (fake regal).
- prefer-string-interpolation is ignored (not warning) in the shared config: its fix changes policy behaviour (undefined sprintf arg vs "<undefined>", %q/%v) and older agents' OPA can't parse template strings.
